"texte": [
"Chancellor, distinguished faculty members, and graduating students, as you prepare to step beyond these university gates into an open and rapidly evolving world, you belong to a generation that enjoys an unprecedented expansion of personal liberty. Never before have young adults possessed such latitude to design their own careers, curate their identities, express dissenting views, and traverse geographic and cultural boundaries. Yet, on this celebratory occasion, I wish to invite you to reflect upon a subtle philosophical paradox that defines our age: the more boundless individual freedom appears on the surface, the more elusive and exhausting it often feels in lived experience.",
"This paradox manifests itself first in what psychologist Barry Schwartz aptly termed the 'tyranny of choice.' Modern consumer capitalism and digital platforms equate freedom with an infinite menu of options—from career paths and romantic partners to lifestyles and algorithmic entertainment feeds. Far from producing serene empowerment, however, hyper-choice frequently induces decision paralysis, chronic regret, and self-blame: when every life outcome is framed as the direct consequence of an unconstrained personal choice, any setback feels like an indictment of one's own judgment.",
"Second, contemporary freedom is increasingly compromised by invisible architectures of persuasion. While democratic citizens rightly guard against overt state censorship or authoritarian coercion, we willingly surrender vast amounts of behavioral data to commercial algorithms engineered to nudge our desires, monopolize our attention, and manufacture artificial needs. As the philosopher Isaiah Berlin warned in his classic distinction between 'negative liberty'—freedom from external interference—and 'positive liberty'—the capacity to be one's own rational master—an individual free from legal chains may still remain enslaved to manipulated impulses.",
"Furthermore, an absolutist conception of personal freedom that denies all mutual limits rapidly becomes self-defeating. When absolute freedom of speech degenerates into coordinated online harassment that silences vulnerable voices, or when unrestricted economic liberty degrades the shared climate upon which all future generations depend, unchecked individual license destroys the very conditions that make freedom possible for everyone.",
"Therefore, graduating class, do not mistake freedom for the mere absence of constraints or the impulsive satisfaction of every passing whim. True freedom is an arduous civic and moral practice: it resides in cultivating critical self-mastery, accepting the ethical boundaries required by human coexistence, and dedicating your talents to building a free and just society for all."
],
