    "texte": [
      "During a televised economic policy forum in Geneva, international trade economist Prof. Julian Mercer and industrial policy scholar Dr. Nadia Benali debated the resurgence of tariffs, import quotas, and economic nationalism across advanced and developing democracies. 'Over the past forty years, rules-based global trade has lifted more than a billion people out of extreme poverty, lowered consumer prices, and accelerated technological diffusion,' Prof. Mercer argued. 'Reverting to protectionist tariffs is an economic self-inflicted wound: it raises input costs for domestic manufacturers, triggers retaliatory trade wars, and historically deepens global stagnation.'",
      "Dr. Benali listened carefully before challenging the assumptions behind classical free-trade orthodoxy: 'Your aggregate statistics capture genuine gains in emerging Asia, Julian, yet they obscure the localized devastation inflicted upon deindustrialized regions in Western democracies and nascent industries in the Global South. When capital moves freely across borders while displaced workers are left without retraining or regional investment, protectionist sentiment is not irrational xenophobia—it is a predictable democratic backlash against uncompensated dislocation.'",
"'I fully agree that governments failed to redistribute the gains of trade fairly to displaced communities,' Prof. Mercer conceded, 'but tariffs are the wrong remedy. Erecting trade walls shields inefficient domestic monopolies from competition, penalizes low-income households who spend a larger share of their income on basic imported goods, and fractures international cooperation needed to tackle climate change.'",
"'Unconditional protectionism certainly breeds inefficiency,' Dr. Benali replied, 'which is why we must distinguish between crude, beggar-thy-neighbor nationalism and targeted, temporary industrial policy. Historically, virtually every industrialized nation—from nineteenth-century Britain and the United States to postwar South Korea—used selective infant-industry protections to build technological capabilities before opening fully to global competition. Similarly, carbon border adjustments are essential today so that countries enforcing strict environmental standards are not undercut by heavily polluting imports.'",
"By the close of their dialogue, both economists converged on a pragmatic middle ground. As Prof. Mercer summarized, 'The sustainable alternative to reckless hyper-globalization is neither autarkic isolation nor blind deregulation, but an embedded trade system that pairs open commerce with robust domestic social safety nets and shared ecological standards.'"
