"texte": [
"JOURNALIST: Professor Lindqvist, unlike earlier waves of industrial mechanization that primarily automated repetitive manual labor on factory floors, today's generative artificial intelligence and advanced robotics increasingly perform cognitive, analytical, and creative tasks. Are we facing an unprecedented wave of technological unemployment?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: History cautions us against simplistic prophecies of mass joblessness, yet it also warns against complacency. Past technological revolutions—from the steam engine to personal computing—ultimately created more jobs than they destroyed, but only after decades of painful social dislocation and institutional adaptation. What distinguishes the current AI revolution is its sheer speed and breadth: algorithms now draft legal briefs, diagnose medical scans, write software code, and manage customer service simultaneously, compressing into years transitions that previously unfolded over generations.",
"JOURNALIST: Which segments of the workforce stand to gain from this transformation, and who faces the greatest risk of displacement?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: We are witnessing a sharp polarization of the labor market. Highly skilled professionals who can harness AI as a cognitive co-pilot—augmenting their strategic judgment, complex problem-solving, and interpersonal leadership—experience surging productivity and compensation. Conversely, routine white-collar administrative roles, entry-level analytical positions, and standardized service jobs face acute substitution pressure, threatening to hollow out the traditional middle-class career ladder.",
"JOURNALIST: Beyond job numbers, how is algorithmic automation altering the daily quality and dignity of human work?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: That is perhaps the most overlooked dimension. In warehouses, call centers, and delivery platforms, algorithmic management increasingly subjects workers to relentless real-time surveillance, automated performance scoring, and accelerated physical or mental pacing. When efficiency is optimized solely for machine metrics, human autonomy, craft pride, and workplace solidarity suffer severe erosion.",
"JOURNALIST: What social and economic policies must governments adopt to ensure that automation promotes shared human flourishing rather than extreme inequality?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: Technology does not dictate our destiny; institutional choices do. First, we must overhaul education toward lifelong learning, critical thinking, and human-centric care professions that machines cannot replicate. Second, governments should tax windfall productivity gains from automation to fund shorter working weeks, robust unemployment insurance, and worker co-determination over how AI is deployed in the workplace. Automation should liberate human beings from drudgery, not render human labor disposable."
],
