      "For more than seventy years, Gross Domestic Product (GDP) growth has functioned as the unquestioned supreme metric of national success across virtually every political ideology. Governments rise or fall based on quarterly expansion figures, while central banks and international institutions treat perpetual compound growth—typically three percent annually, which doubles economic output every twenty-four years—as the indispensable precondition for employment, fiscal solvency, and social peace. Yet in an era of accelerating ecological overshoot, the dogma of infinite material growth on a finite planet confronts an inescapable biophysical contradiction.",
      "Ecological economists have long demonstrated that the economy is not an isolated perpetual-motion machine suspended in a vacuum, but an open subsystem embedded within the Earth's biosphere. Every unit of conventional economic output requires material throughput—energy, minerals, timber, freshwater, and fertile soil—and generates waste sinks in the form of greenhouse gases, chemical pollutants, and biodiversity loss. While proponents of 'green growth' contend that technological innovation can decouple GDP expansion from environmental degradation, empirical evidence indicates that absolute, global, and sufficiently rapid decoupling remains elusive whenever total consumption continues to climb.",
