"JOURNALIST: Dr. Okafor, over the past century, antibiotics, vaccines, and intensive-care technologies fostered a widespread conviction that modern biomedicine had definitively conquered infectious disease. Why has the accelerating emergence of novel zoonotic pathogens—from Ebola and SARS to avian influenza and antimicrobial-resistant superbugs—shattered that triumphalist assumption?\nDR. OKAFOR: Because twentieth-century medicine excelled at treating isolated pathogens inside individual hospital patients, yet remained largely blind to the ecological and social systems driving spillover events. Over seventy percent of emerging infectious diseases originate in wildlife before jumping to humans—a process accelerated by tropical deforestation, industrial factory farming, wildlife trafficking, and global air travel. When humanity disrupts biodiverse ecosystems, we dismantle natural buffers and bring novel viruses directly into dense urban populations.",
"JOURNALIST: Beyond ecological disruption, what internal limitations within modern healthcare systems hinder our response to emerging outbreaks?\nDR. OKAFOR: Our dominant biomedical model is overwhelmingly reactive and hospital-centric rather than preventive and community-based. Pharmaceutical markets invest heavily in lucrative chronic medications for wealthy consumers while underfunding broad-spectrum antivirals, new antibiotic classes, and surveillance laboratories in low-income regions where outbreaks frequently begin. Furthermore, during global emergencies, vaccine nationalism and intellectual-property hoarding repeatedly delay equitable distribution—allowing viruses to mutate unchecked in under-vaccinated populations.",
