    "texte": [
      "Whenever governments propose binding carbon pricing, phase-outs of fossil-fuel subsidies, or stricter industrial emissions standards, a familiar rhetorical refrain immediately dominates public debate: that ambitious climate action is an unaffordable luxury that will cripple economic competitiveness, eliminate industrial jobs, and depress living standards. Framing planetary survival and economic prosperity as zero-sum adversaries has paralyzed decisive climate policy for over three decades. Yet, examined through the lens of modern ecological economics, this alleged trade-off rests upon a profound accounting illusion—one that systematically mistakes short-term private profit for genuine long-term wealth.",
      "Conventional macroeconomic models have historically treated the Earth's atmosphere, freshwater aquifers, and stable climate patterns as free, limitless externalities whose degradation carries zero cost on corporate balance sheets. As economist Nicholas Stern famously concluded in his landmark review of climate economics, climate change represents the greatest market failure the world has ever seen. When the real costs of catastrophic floods, prolonged droughts, crop failures, heat-induced labor productivity losses, and coastal infrastructure destruction are factored in, unmitigated planetary warming inflicts economic damages orders of magnitude greater than the investments required to decarbonize today.",
