"texte": [
"Mr. President, Excellencies, and distinguished delegates to the United Nations Assembly on Planetary Commons, for over two centuries modern industrial civilization has operated under an unspoken territorial assumption: that natural resources—whether subterranean minerals, tropical rainforests, freshwater basins, or deep-sea ecosystems—are inert commodities awaiting unrestricted extraction by whoever commands the capital and technology to claim them. Today, as global material extraction has tripled over the past fifty years and pushed Earth's life-support systems toward irreversible tipping points, we must confront an inescapable moral and geopolitical truth: how humanity governs Earth's finite resources is no longer a narrow commercial question, but the supreme test of our collective responsibility.",
"Nowhere is the injustice of unbridled resource exploitation more glaring than in the persistent asymmetry between extraction and consumption. Wealthy industrialized nations consume nearly five times more material resources per capita than low-income countries, yet the ecological devastation—toxic tailings ponds, polluted rivers, displaced rural communities, and ravaged forests—is overwhelmingly offshored to resource-rich developing nations. Meanwhile, weak transparency laws and opaque corporate supply chains allow illicit resource extraction to finance armed conflicts and deprive local populations of fair fiscal revenues.",
"Furthermore, treating nature purely as an inexhaustible quarry ignores the vital ecological services that intact ecosystems perform for all humanity. The Amazon and Congo rainforest basins, high-altitude glaciers, and coastal wetlands regulate global rainfall, stabilize climate patterns, and sustain agricultural fertility far beyond national borders.",
"Exercising collective responsibility does not mean denying developing nations their legitimate right to utilize their natural endowments for poverty eradication and sustainable industrialization. Rather, it requires forging a new global compact grounded in circularity, equity, and planetary stewardship.",
"Therefore, delegates, let us enact binding international supply-chain due diligence laws, compensate forest-bearing nations for preserving global climate sinks, transition from linear 'extract-use-discard' production toward circular regenerative economies, and affirm that Earth's natural wealth is a shared inheritance held in trust for all humanity."
],
