"texte": [
"Covering over seventy percent of the Earth's surface, generating half of the oxygen we breathe, and absorbing more than ninety percent of the excess heat trapped by anthropogenic greenhouse gases, the global ocean has quietly served as humanity's primary climatic shock absorber. Yet, reporting on marine science and maritime policy as an environmental columnist over the past fifteen years, I have watched this vast blue expanse pushed toward an unprecedented ecological crisis. Out of sight beneath the waves, industrial overfishing, chemical and plastic pollution, marine heatwaves, and ocean acidification are dismantling marine ecosystems at an alarming pace.",
"The scale of industrial extraction at sea defies easy comprehension. Subsidized industrial trawler fleets equipped with sonar and satellite tracking now sweep ocean floors clean, depleting over a third of global fish stocks beyond biologically sustainable limits and threatening the food security of over three billion people who rely on seafood as a primary protein source. Simultaneously, millions of tons of plastic waste and agricultural fertilizer runoff pour into coastal waters annually, choking marine wildlife with microplastics and creating vast oxygen-depleted 'dead zones' where aquatic life suffocates.",
"Compounding these direct assaults is the invisible chemical transformation of seawater itself: as oceans absorb rising atmospheric carbon dioxide, seawater becomes progressively more acidic, dissolving the calcium carbonate shells of corals, oysters, and microscopic plankton that form the base of the entire marine food web.",
"Now, a new frontier of potential devastation looms in the abyssal depths: proposed deep-sea mining operations seeking polymetallic nodules for battery manufacturing risk obliterating fragile, slow-growing ecosystems before scientists have even cataloged their species.",
"Nevertheless, the adoption of the landmark UN High Seas Treaty offers a historic turning point if nations muster the political will to enforce it. By placing at least thirty percent of the world's oceans under strict marine protection by 2030, halting destructive bottom trawling, declaring a precautionary moratorium on deep-sea mining, and curbing plastic production at its source, humanity can allow the ocean's extraordinary capacity for self-regeneration to flourish once more."
],
