"JOURNALIST: Professor Vance, from European integration debates to trade disputes and climate treaties, the slogan 'taking back control' has become a potent rallying cry across modern politics. Has economic and digital globalization rendered traditional national sovereignty obsolete?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Not obsolete, but profoundly transformed. Classical seventeenth-century Westphalian sovereignty imagined each state as a hermetically sealed legal fortress exercising absolute internal and external autonomy. In an age where capital, greenhouse gases, pandemics, and cross-border data flows traverse national frontiers in milliseconds, no single state—not even a superpower—can safeguard its citizens' prosperity or security in unilateral isolation. Attempting to exercise absolute solo sovereignty today often produces the illusion of control alongside the reality of impotence.",
