"Walking through the vaulted corridors of the Palais des Nations in Geneva, one senses both the noble idealism that birthed modern multilateralism after 1945 and the acute paralysis currently gripping global governance. Covering international organizations as a diplomatic columnist over the past fifteen years, I have watched the post-war institutional architecture—centered on the United Nations Security Council, the World Trade Organization, and the Bretton Woods financial institutions—struggle repeatedly to prevent brutal wars, resolve sovereign debt crises, or enforce binding climate commitments.",
