    "texte": [
      "Corruption is frequently caricatured in public commentary as a localized moral failing—the isolated greed of a bribed customs official, an embezzling minister, or a crooked procurement officer. Yet decades of comparative political economy reveal that entrenched corruption is far more insidious than occasional individual misconduct: it is a systemic pathology that corrodes the foundational social contract between citizens and the state. Whenever public office is systematically exploited for private enrichment, the invisible currency of institutional trust evaporates, replaced by pervasive cynicism, legal nihilism, and social fragmentation.",
      "The economic and human toll of systemic corruption is staggering. When public infrastructure contracts are awarded through kickbacks rather than transparent merit, roads crumble prematurely, hospital budgets are siphoned away, and environmental regulations are waived for polluting enterprises. Crucially, corruption acts as a deeply regressive tax: wealthy elites can afford bribes to expedite permits or purchase private alternatives, whereas low-income families depend entirely on intact public schools, clinics, and courts—and suffer first when public funds are plundered.",
"Moreover, institutional trust erosion is not triggered solely by illegal bribery; 'legalized corruption'—such as opaque campaign financing, revolving-door lobbying between regulators and corporations, and offshore tax shelters—inflicts equally severe damage on democratic legitimacy by convincing ordinary citizens that rules are rigged in favor of entrenched insiders.",
"Once institutional trust collapses, societies enter a perilous vicious cycle: citizens evade taxes because they assume officials will steal public funds, while underfunded states grow ever weaker and more vulnerable to authoritarian strongmen promising ruthless order.",
