"texte": [
"JOURNALIST: Professor Mensah, many emerging economies across Asia, Africa, and Latin America boast dynamic young populations, abundant natural resources, and rapid urbanization, yet struggle to translate economic growth into broad-based human development. Why do you place 'state capacity and governance quality' at the very center of development economics?\nPROFESSOR MENSAH: Because economic resources alone never automatically generate shared prosperity; institutions determine whether national wealth builds schools and railways or disappears into offshore accounts. As economists Daron Acemoglu and James Robinson demonstrated in 'Why Nations Fail,' nations thrive when they build 'inclusive institutions'—impartial courts, meritocratic civil services, transparent tax systems, and accountable parliaments—rather than 'extractive institutions' designed to enrich a narrow political and economic elite.",
"JOURNALIST: What specific administrative bottlenecks most frequently undermine public policy implementation in rapidly growing emerging economies?\nPROFESSOR MENSAH: A major obstacle is the gap between ambitious legislative plans and weak local administrative execution. Many emerging states suffer from under-resourced municipal governments, fragmented civil-service recruitment, and cumbersome bureaucratic procedures that both deter formal small-business investment and create opportunities for petty bribery. Simultaneously, low domestic tax-to-GDP ratios—often due to large informal sectors and generous tax exemptions for multinational corporations—leave governments chronically short of predictable revenue to finance universal healthcare and quality education.",
"JOURNALIST: International financial institutions have often prescribed standardized governance templates modeled on Western bureaucracies. Why have those externally imposed reforms frequently faltered?\nPROFESSOR MENSAH: Because institutional legitimacy cannot be imported like turnkey machinery; when reforms are dictated as top-down loan conditions without local ownership or adaptation to cultural and social realities, they often produce 'isomorphic mimicry'—governments adopt the superficial forms of modern laws and anti-corruption agencies while actual power continues operating through informal patronage networks.",
"JOURNALIST: Which homegrown governance innovations offer the most promising path forward for emerging economies today?\nPROFESSOR MENSAH: We are seeing remarkable homegrown breakthroughs: digital public infrastructure that streamlines tax collection and delivers direct cash transfers without middlemen, participatory local governance councils that monitor public works, and independent audit commissions empowered by active civil societies. Sustainable development flourishes when states build capable, accountable institutions rooted in their own citizens' aspirations."
