    "texte": [
      "Inside a modest wooden house overlooking a mist-shrouded highland valley, eighty-four-year-old Kofi Mensah spoke softly into a digital microphone while twenty-six-year-old linguist Dr. Amina Diallo carefully transcribed every tonal inflection into her notebook. Kofi was one of only twelve remaining fluent speakers of an ancestral forest language whose vocabulary contained over four hundred distinct terms for medicinal plants, seasonal wind shifts, and ecological kinship. As the afternoon rain drummed gently on the tin roof, Amina realized she was not merely documenting grammatical structures; she was racing against time to rescue an entire irreplaceable universe of human perception before silence claimed it forever.",
      "Across the globe, an unprecedented linguistic extinction is unfolding quietly out of public view. According to UNESCO estimates, roughly forty percent of the world's seven thousand living languages are currently endangered, with one language falling silent approximately every two weeks. Driven by standardized national education systems, rural-to-urban migration, digital media dominated by a handful of global lingua francas, and the lingering stigma of colonial linguistic hierarchies, younger generations increasingly abandon ancestral tongues in favor of dominant commercial languages.",
