    "texte": [
      "Madam Chairperson, Ministers of Education, dedicated teachers, and student representatives gathered for this World Forum on Educational Equity, throughout modern history societies have placed immense hope in public schooling as the great equalizer—the engine capable of dismantling inherited privilege and unlocking human potential regardless of birth. Yet as we examine contemporary educational systems across both wealthy and developing nations, we must confront a sobering paradox: while access to classrooms has expanded dramatically, schools too often reproduce existing social inequalities rather than transforming them.",
      "As the French sociologist Pierre Bourdieu famously demonstrated, educational institutions frequently mistake inherited cultural capital—books at home, academic vocabulary, museum visits, and parental familiarity with institutional codes—for innate individual merit. When underfunded public schools serving working-class or rural communities suffer from overcrowded classrooms, teacher shortages, and outdated facilities while affluent families purchase elite private tutoring and privileged university pathways, education risks legitimizing social stratification behind a veneer of meritocratic fairness.",
"Moreover, reducing education solely to utilitarian workforce training—teaching young people merely how to pass standardized tests or operate current software—betrays its deeper civic and emancipatory vocation. As the Brazilian educator Paulo Freire argued in 'Pedagogy of the Oppressed,' true education cannot operate as a passive 'banking model' in which teachers deposit pre-packaged facts into compliant minds; rather, it must cultivate critical consciousness, empowering learners to question injustice and reimagine their society.",
"Realizing education's transformative power therefore requires bold structural investment alongside pedagogical renewal: allocating disproportionate public funding to disadvantaged schools, elevating the salaries and professional autonomy of teachers, and integrating critical thinking, civic ethics, and ecological literacy into every curriculum.",
"Therefore, delegates, let us refuse to treat education merely as an economic commodity or a sorting mechanism for the labor market. By investing wholeheartedly in universal, emancipatory public education, we equip every child with the intellectual freedom and moral agency required to build a fairer, more humane world."
