"texte": [
"JOURNALIST: Professor Bakker, for much of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, monolingual nation-states frequently treated bilingualism or multilingualism in children as a source of cognitive confusion or divided civic loyalty. How has contemporary psycholinguistics overturned that outdated prejudice?\nPROFESSOR BAKKER: Completely and decisively. Decades of neuroimaging and behavioral studies demonstrate that navigating multiple languages actually strengthens the brain's 'executive control network'—the prefrontal system responsible for selective attention, task switching, mental flexibility, and inhibiting distractions. Far from confusing young minds, juggling two or more linguistic systems acts as a lifelong cognitive workout that enhances metalinguistic awareness and even delays the onset of age-related neurodegenerative symptoms by several years.",
"JOURNALIST: Beyond neurological advantages, how does speaking multiple languages enrich interpersonal empathy and cross-cultural understanding?\nPROFESSOR BAKKER: Every language embodies a distinct conceptual lens for categorizing emotions, social relationships, and moral nuances. When individuals learn to express themselves across languages, they discover that their native culture's way of naming reality is not the only valid perspective. Psycholinguistic experiments consistently show that multilingual children and adults develop stronger 'theory of mind'—the ability to step outside their own egocentric viewpoint and grasp what another person knows, feels, or intends.",
"JOURNALIST: Yet in many educational systems, prestige is still reserved exclusively for elite international languages like English, while the home languages of immigrant or indigenous students are stigmatized. How does this linguistic hierarchy affect society?\nPROFESSOR BAKKER: It inflicts a tragic double loss: it undermines minority students' self-esteem and academic confidence while squandering a priceless societal asset. Valuing 'additive bilingualism'—where schools nurture a child's home language alongside national and international languages—boosts overall academic achievement and strengthens social cohesion across diverse communities.",
"JOURNALIST: In an era of instant AI translation earbuds and real-time text translators, some argue that learning foreign languages is becoming obsolete. Why do you disagree?\nPROFESSOR BAKKER: Machine translation is a useful logistical convenience for ordering a meal or translating a manual, yet it can never replace genuine human language mastery. Trust, humor, poetic subtlety, and deep diplomatic or personal rapport are forged when we make the respectful effort to meet another human being inside their own linguistic world. Multilingualism is not a technical skill replaceable by software; it is an irreplaceable bridge of human solidarity."
],
